* The K-Armed Bandit Problem: Formal Definition

- @Definition@: The **K-Armed Bandit** problem is defined as:
  - There are $K$ actions (aka "arms"), indexed $i = 1, \ldots, K$
  - Time horizon: $T$ rounds (sometimes $T = \infty$)
  - At each round $t$
    1. Agent selects arm $A_t \in \{1, \ldots, K\}$
    2. Environment reveals reward $R_t \sim P_{A_t}$
    3. Agent observes only $R_t$ (not rewards from other arms)

- @Assumptions@: **Stochastic bandit** model
  - Each arm $i$ has a fixed reward distribution $P_i$ with mean
    $\mu_i = \EE[R | A = i]$
  - Rewards drawn from a _given_ arm are iid across the rounds that arm is
    pulled, and independent across arms
    - Note that $R_1, R_2, \ldots$ is _not_ an iid sequence, since the law of
      $R_t$ depends on which arm $A_t$ was chosen
  - The distributions $P_i$ and their means $\mu_1, \ldots, \mu_K$ are unknown
    to the agent

- @Goal@: Design a policy (algorithm) that selects $A_1, A_2, \ldots, A_T$ to
  maximize the total reward $\sum_{t=1}^{T} R_t$

* Reward Distributions

- @Definition@: Each arm $i$ has an associated **reward distribution** $P_i$
  - Common choices for $P_i$ are Bernoulli, Gaussian, or any distribution with
    bounded support
  - When arm $i$ is pulled, reward $R \sim P_i$ is sampled

- @Definition@: The **expected reward** for an arm is $\mu_i = \EE[R | A = i]$
  - This is the quantity we care about for maximizing total reward

- @Example@: **Bernoulli Bandits**
  - Each arm $i$ returns reward $R_t = 1$ with probability $\mu_i$, else
    $R_t = 0$
  - $\mu_i$ is the "success probability" or "conversion rate"
  - E.g., in A/B testing $\mu_i$ is click-through rate of ad $i$

- @Challenges@: Parameters of the arms are **unknown**
  - The means $\mu_1, \ldots, \mu_K$ are unknown
  - Also variance and shape of distribution are typically unknown
  - Agent must estimate these from observed rewards
  - Must balance learning (exploration) and earning (exploitation)

// https://raw.githack.com/gpsaggese/gpsaggese.github.io/gp_scratch/msml610/tutorials/L09_multi_armed_bandits/L09_03_02_multi_armed_bandits.html#Cell-10:-Regret-Accumulation

* Performance Metrics

- @Question@: How do we measure algorithm performance when rewards are random?

- @Definition@: The **optimal arm** is the arm with highest expected reward
  $$i^* = \argmax_i \mu_i, \qquad \mu^* \defeq \mu_{i^*} = \max_i \mu_i$$
  - If we knew $\mu_1, \ldots, \mu_K$, we would always pull arm $i^*$

- @Definition@: The **instantaneous regret** at time $t$ is the difference
  between optimal mean and mean of chosen arm
  $$\ell_t = \mu^* - \mu_{A_t} \geq 0$$
  - _Intuition_: How much expected reward we "lose" by not playing $i^*$

- @Definition@: The **cumulative pseudo-regret** over $T$ rounds:
  $$L_T = \sum_{t=1}^{T} \ell_t = T \mu^* - \sum_{t=1}^{T} \mu_{A_t}$$
  - "Pseudo" because it uses the _means_ $\mu_{A_t}$, not the realized rewards
    $R_t$
  - $L_T$ is still random, since the arms $A_t$ the policy picks are random
  - _Goal_: Design an algorithm with low $\EE[L_T]$

* Performance Metrics: Intuition

- @Question@: Why regret? Why expectation?
  - Cannot maximize _reward with certainty_ due to randomness in rewards
    - One arm can look better than its $\mu_i$ due to luck
    - Even optimal policy can get unlucky low reward on a pull
  - But can minimize _expected loss_ relative to optimal strategy
    - "Expected" is the key word (vs actual)
  - Averaging out the reward noise isolates the only thing the policy controls,
    which is _which arm it pulls_

- @Properties@: Pseudo-regret depends only on how many times we pull each arm
  - Let $N_i(T) = \sum_{t=1}^{T} I[A_t = i]$ = "number of times arm $i$ pulled",
    then
    $$
    L_T
      = \sum_{i=1}^{K} (\mu^* - \mu_i) N_i(T)
      = \sum_{i=1}^{K} \Delta_i \, N_i(T)
    $$
    where $\Delta_i = \mu^* - \mu_i$ is the "suboptimality gap" of arm $i$

- @Implications@
  - To minimize regret, pull suboptimal arms as few times as possible
    - But we don't know which arms are suboptimal initially!
    - Must explore to identify $i^*$, but exploration increases regret
  - Regret is the "opportunity cost" of learning

* Regret in Practice

- @Problem@: Regret cannot be computed on a deployed system
  - $L_T$ depends on $\mu^*$ and on the gaps $\Delta_i$, which are unknown
  - Knowing them would make the bandit problem trivial

- @Key idea@: Regret is an _analysis_ tool, not a _monitoring_ tool
  - Use it to compare algorithms in theory and in simulation
  - Use proxies in production

- @Techniques@: what practitioners monitor instead
  - _Realized reward rate_: $\frac{1}{t}\sum_{\tau \le t} R_\tau$, compared to a
    held-out uniform control slice
  - _Arm share_: fraction of traffic each arm receives over time
    - A healthy run concentrates on few arms, but never to exactly 0%
  - _Off-policy estimates_: replay logged data under a candidate policy
  - _Simulation_: replay with synthetic arms whose $\mu_i$ you set, so that
    $L_T$ _is_ computable

- @Remark@: keeping a small uniform-random slice of traffic is common
  - Costs a little regret, buys unbiased data for offline evaluation

# Algorithms

// https://raw.githack.com/gpsaggese/gpsaggese.github.io/gp_scratch/msml610/tutorials/L09_multi_armed_bandits/L09_03_02_multi_armed_bandits.html#Cell-3:-Greedy-Algorithm-Failure

* Greedy Algorithm

- @Definition@: **Greedy Algorithm**
  1. _Initialization_: pull each arm once, for $t = 1, \ldots, K$
  2. For $t > K$, compute the empirical mean reward for each arm
     $$\hat{\mu}_i(t) = \frac{1}{N_i(t)} \sum_{\tau < t: A_\tau = i} R_\tau$$
  3. Pull arm with highest empirical mean: $A_t = \argmax_i \hat{\mu}_i(t)$
  - _Pure exploitation_: always pull current best arm

- @Remark@: the initialization step is not optional
  - Without it $\hat{\mu}_i(t) = 0/0$ for an arm that was never pulled
  - Equivalent conventions: set $\hat{\mu}_i = +\infty$ until arm $i$ is pulled

- @Limitations@
  - _Problem_: May get "stuck" on suboptimal arm
  - _Example_:
    - Suppose arm 1 has $\mu_1 = 0.6$, arm 2 has $\mu_2 = 0.7$
    - Initialization gives arm 1 reward 1 and arm 2 reward 0
    - Then $\hat{\mu}_1 = 1 > 0 = \hat{\mu}_2$, and we keep pulling arm 1
    - Never revisit arm 2, which is actually better
  - _Regret_: $L_T = \Omega(T)$, linear regret (very bad!)

* Uniform Exploration

- @Definition@: **Uniform Exploration** (aka "random selection"), at each time
  $t$ choose $A_t$ uniformly at random from $\{1, \ldots, K\}$
  - _Pure exploration_: no use of observed rewards

- @Limitations@:
  - _Problem_: Never uses information from rewards
    - Pulls all arms equally, even after learning which are bad
  - _Regret_: expected instantaneous regret is the constant
    $\frac{1}{K}\sum_{i=1}^{K} \Delta_i$ in _every_ round, so
    $$L_T = \Theta(T)$$
    which is linear, as bad as greedy

- @Remark@: greedy and uniform exploration fail for _opposite_ reasons
  - Greedy commits too early on too little data
  - Uniform exploration never commits at all
  - Both give linear regret, so a good algorithm must interpolate between them

* Explore-Then-Commit

- @Definition@: **Explore-Then-Commit (ETC)** with exploration budget $n$
  1. _Explore_: pull each arm exactly $n$ times ($nK$ rounds total)
  2. _Commit_: play $\argmax_i \hat{\mu}_i$ for all remaining $T - nK$ rounds

- @Remark@: ETC _is_ a classic A/B test
  - Run a fixed-size experiment, then ship the winner
  - The exploration phase is the "test", the commit phase is the "rollout"

- @Properties@: regret has two competing terms
  $$
  \EE[L_T]
    \le \underbrace{n \sum_{i} \Delta_i}_{\text{cost of exploring}}
      + \underbrace{T \sum_{i} \Delta_i \exp(-n \Delta_i^2 / 4)}_
        {\text{cost of committing to the wrong arm}}
  $$
  - Small $n$: cheap exploration, high chance of committing to a bad arm
  - Large $n$: safe commitment, but exploration itself costs a lot

- @Limitations@
  - With $n$ fixed and $T$ growing, the second term makes $L_T = \Omega(T)$
  - Tuning $n \approx (4/\Delta^2)\log(T\Delta^2/4)$ needs the unknown $\Delta$
  - Best _gap-free_ tuning gives only $\EE[L_T] = \Theta(T^{2/3} K^{1/3})$
  - The split into "learn" then "earn" is exactly what adaptive algorithms avoid

// https://raw.githack.com/gpsaggese/gpsaggese.github.io/gp_scratch/msml610/tutorials/L09_multi_armed_bandits/L09_03_02_multi_armed_bandits.html#Cell-5:-Epsilon-Greedy-Algorithm

* $\epsilon$-Greedy Algorithm

- @Key idea@: Need adaptive strategy that interleaves exploration and
  exploitation instead of separating them like ETC
  - Greedy algorithm: too much exploitation
  - Uniform exploration: too much exploration

- @Definition@: **$\epsilon$-Greedy Algorithm** explores with small probability
  $\epsilon$, otherwise exploits

- @Algorithm@: at each time $t$:
    1. Compute empirical means:
       $$
       \hat{\mu}_i(t)
       = \frac{1}{N_i(t)} \sum_{\tau < t: A_\tau = i} R_\tau
       $$
    2. With probability $\epsilon$: Choose arm uniformly at random (explore)
    3. With probability $1 - \epsilon$: Choose arm $\argmax_i \hat{\mu}_i(t)$
       (exploit)
  - $\epsilon \in (0, 1)$ is a hyperparameter
    - Small $\epsilon$ (e.g., 0.01): mostly exploit, little exploration
    - Large $\epsilon$ (e.g., 0.5): explore half the time
    - Typical choice: $\epsilon = 0.1$

* $\epsilon$-Greedy: Exploration Schedules

- @Variants@: two families of exploration schedules

- **Fixed schedule**: use the same $\epsilon$ for all $t$
  - _Problem_: Keeps exploring at a constant rate forever, even after the best
    arm is identified
  - _Regret_: $L_T = \Theta(T)$, linear, since each round pays an expected
    $\frac{\epsilon}{K}\sum_i \Delta_i$

- **Decaying schedule**: use a time-varying $\epsilon_t$ that decreases with $t$
  - _Intuition_: Explore a lot early, less over time
  - _Schedule_ $\epsilon_t = O(t^{-1/3})$: gives $L_T = O(T^{2/3})$
    - Gap-free, i.e., needs no knowledge of the $\Delta_i$
  - _Schedule_ $\epsilon_t = \min(1, cK / (d^2 t))$: gives $L_T = O(\log T)$
    - Auer, Cesa-Bianchi, Fischer (2002), Theorem 3
    - Requires a known lower bound $d \leq \min_{i: \Delta_i > 0} \Delta_i$
  - If the decay is too slow: too much exploration $\to$ higher regret
  - If the decay is too fast: may never correct an early mistake

- @Pros@
  - Simple, easy to implement, works reasonably well in practice

- @Cons@
  - The $O(\log T)$ schedule needs the unknown gap $d$, so it is not adaptive
  - Explores uniformly over arms, wasting pulls on clearly bad arms
  - UCB and Thompson Sampling get $O(\log T)$ with no such knowledge

* Other Simple Exploration Heuristics

- @Optimistic Initialization@: initialize $\hat{\mu}_i(0)$ to a value above any
  achievable reward, then run pure greedy
  - E.g., for rewards in $[0, 1]$ pretend each arm already returned $m$ rewards
    equal to 1
  - Every pull _lowers_ an optimistic estimate, so greedy is forced to cycle
    through all arms before settling
  - _Pros_: one line of code, no randomization
  - _Cons_: exploration stops permanently once estimates drop, so it fails
    under non-stationarity
  - Same "optimism" idea that UCB makes rigorous, see next section

- @Softmax / Boltzmann Exploration@: sample arm $i$ with probability
  $$
  \Pr(A_t = i)
    = \frac{\exp(\hat{\mu}_i(t) / \tau)}
           {\sum_{j=1}^{K} \exp(\hat{\mu}_j(t) / \tau)}
  $$
  where $\tau > 0$ is a "temperature"
  - $\tau \to 0$: greedy, $\tau \to \infty$: uniform exploration
  - _Pros_: explores in proportion to how good an arm looks, unlike
    $\epsilon$-greedy which explores uniformly
  - _Cons_: ignores _uncertainty_, only uses the point estimate, and $\tau$ must
    be tuned or annealed
  - Same softmax that reappears as the policy parameterization in RL (L12.1)

# Regret Lower Bounds

* Regret Lower Bounds: Why They Matter

- @Question@: Why do lower bounds for algorithms matter?

- @Answers@
  - Lower bounds show fundamental limits of _any_ algorithm
    - No algorithm can do better (up to constants)
  - Tells us if an algorithm is near-optimal
  - Guides algorithm design: what's achievable?

- @Remark@: we have seen 3 algorithms with linear regret (greedy, uniform, fixed
  $\epsilon$) and 1 with $O(T^{2/3})$ (ETC, decaying $\epsilon$)
  - Natural question: how low can regret go?

* Regret Lower Bounds: Can Regret Be Constant?

- @Question@: Can regret be constant, i.e., can
  $$
  L_T \defeq \sum_{t=1}^{T} \ell_t = \sum_{i=1}^{K} \Delta_i N_i(T)
  $$
  stop growing as $T$ grows?

- @Answer@: No, the intuition is
  - Need to distinguish between arms with close means
    - If $\mu_1 \approx \mu_2$, need many samples to tell which is better
  - Must pull suboptimal arms enough to be confident they're suboptimal
  - Any algorithm that stops pulling an arm after finitely many pulls can be
    fooled by a problem instance where that arm is secretly the best
  - Trade-off: confidence vs regret

* Regret Lower Bounds: Lai-Robbins Bound

- @Theorem@: **Lai and Robbins (1985)**:
  - For any "consistent" algorithm (i.e., one whose regret is sub-polynomial on
    every instance)
  - The expected cumulative regret must satisfy
    $$
    \liminf_{T \to \infty} \frac{\EE[L_T]}{\log T} \geq
    \sum_{i: \Delta_i > 0} \frac{\Delta_i}{D(P_i \| P^*)}
    $$
    where $D(P_i \| P^*)$ is the KL divergence between the reward distribution
    of arm $i$ and that of the optimal arm

- @Remark@: for Bernoulli arms the distributions are indexed by their means, so
  $D(P_i \| P^*)$ is often written $D(\mu_i \| \mu^*)$
  - This is an abuse of notation that only makes sense for one-parameter
    families

- @Corollary@: simplified version for bounded rewards:
  $$
  \EE[L_T] = \Omega\left( \sum_{i: \Delta_i > 0} \frac{\log T}{\Delta_i} \right)
  $$
  - Regret must grow at least logarithmically with $T$ ($\Omega$ is a lower
    bound)

- @Interpretation@: cannot achieve constant regret, must pay $\Omega(\log T)$

* Two Regimes: Gap-Dependent vs Minimax Regret

- @Problem@: the bound $\log T / \Delta_i$ blows up as $\Delta_i \to 0$, but
  regret clearly cannot, since $\Delta_i \to 0$ means arm $i$ is _harmless_

- @Key idea@: per-arm regret is capped by the smaller of two terms
  $$\Delta_i \, \EE[N_i(T)] = O\left(\min\left(\Delta_i T,
    \frac{\log T}{\Delta_i}\right)\right)$$
  - Easy arms (large $\Delta_i$): identified fast, cost $\log T / \Delta_i$
  - Hard arms (small $\Delta_i$): never identified, but cost $\Delta_i T$ is
    small anyway
  - Worst case at $\Delta_i \approx \sqrt{K / T}$, where both terms are
    $\approx \sqrt{KT}$

- @Comparison@: two ways to state a regret bound
  - _Gap-dependent (instance-dependent)_: $O(\sum_i \log T / \Delta_i)$
    - Sharp for a _fixed_ instance, vacuous as $\Delta_i \to 0$
  - _Gap-free (minimax, worst-case)_: $\sup_{P_1, \ldots, P_K} \EE[L_T]$
    - UCB1 achieves $O(\sqrt{KT \log T})$, MOSS achieves $O(\sqrt{KT})$

- @Theorem@: **minimax lower bound**: for any algorithm there is a $K$-armed
  instance with $\EE[L_T] = \Omega(\sqrt{KT})$
  - Both bounds are real, they answer different questions
  - _Warning_: $\sqrt{KT}$ is the _best possible_ worst-case rate, not the rate
    of a naive algorithm

* Regret Lower Bounds: Implications for Algorithm Design

- @Goal@: Achieve $O(\log T)$ gap-dependent regret
  - Matches the Lai-Robbins lower bound (up to constants)
  - Called "order-optimal" or "asymptotically optimal"

- @Key idea@: Must pull each suboptimal arm $i$ roughly
  $O\left(\frac{\log T}{\Delta_i^2}\right)$ times
  - This is how many samples are needed to separate $\mu_i$ from $\mu^*$
  - Arms with smaller gaps $\Delta_i$ need more exploration
  - Total regret:
    $$
    \sum_{i: \Delta_i > 0}
      \Delta_i \cdot O\left(\frac{\log T}{\Delta_i^2}\right)
      = O\left( \sum_{i: \Delta_i > 0} \frac{\log T}{\Delta_i} \right)
    $$

- @Limitations@: what won't work
  - _Fixed exploration budget_ (ETC with fixed $n$): commits to the wrong arm
    with constant probability, so $\Omega(T)$
  - _Uniform exploration_: constant per-round regret, so $\Theta(T)$
  - _Fixed $\epsilon$_: constant exploration rate forever, so $\Theta(T)$
  - Need _adaptive_ exploration that concentrates on plausibly-optimal arms

# Optimism and UCB

// https://raw.githack.com/gpsaggese/gpsaggese.github.io/gp_scratch/msml610/tutorials/L09_multi_armed_bandits/L09_03_02_multi_armed_bandits.html#Cell-6:-Confidence-Intervals-for-Each-Arm

* Optimism in the Face of Uncertainty

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
- @Key Idea@: **"Be optimistic about uncertain options"**
  - If unsure about arm's value, assume it could be the best
  - Optimism drives exploration of uncertain arms
  - As arms are explored, uncertainty shrinks

- @Definition@: the **upper confidence bound** for arm $i$ after $n$ pulls is
  $$U_i = \hat{\mu}_i + c_i(n), \quad c_i(n) = \sqrt{\frac{2 \log t}{n}}$$
  - $c_i(n)$ is the _uncertainty radius_, chosen so that $\mu_i \le U_i$ with
    high probability
  - Fewer samples $n$ $\to$ larger $c_i(n)$ $\to$ more optimistic
  - More samples $n$ $\to$ smaller $c_i(n)$ $\to$ less optimistic

- @Intuition@: **Why optimism works**
  - If an arm _is_ optimal: optimism ensures we keep trying it
  - If an arm _is not_ optimal: we learn this and stop pulling it
  - Balances exploration and exploitation with no explicit randomization

- @Comparison@
  - $\epsilon$-greedy: random exploration, ignores uncertainty
  - Optimism: directed exploration toward uncertain arms
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}

