    Learn -> Suboptimal [label="  Bad arm",  color="#C0736F",
                         fontcolor="#C0736F"];
    { rank=same; Optimal; Suboptimal; }
}
```
::::
:::

// https://raw.githack.com/gpsaggese/gpsaggese.github.io/gp_scratch/msml610/tutorials/L09_multi_armed_bandits/L09_03_02_multi_armed_bandits.html#Cell-7:-Upper-Confidence-Bound-(UCB)-Intuition

* Upper Confidence Bound (UCB1)

- @Definition@: **UCB1 Algorithm** (Auer, Cesa-Bianchi, Fischer, 2002)
  - For the first $K$ rounds, pull each arm once
  - For round $t > K$:
    1. For each arm $i$, compute the **UCB index**
       $$
       U_i(t)
       = \underbrace{\hat{\mu}_i(t)}_{\text{exploitation}}
       + \underbrace{\sqrt{\frac{2 \log t}{N_i(t)}}}_{\text{exploration bonus}}
       $$
       where
       - $\hat{\mu}_i(t)$: empirical mean of arm $i$ before round $t$
       - $N_i(t)$: number of times arm $i$ was pulled before round $t$
    2. Pull arm $A_t = \argmax_i U_i(t)$, breaking ties by lowest index

- @Remark@: the bonus uses the _current round_ $t$, not the horizon $T$
  - So UCB1 is _anytime_: it needs no knowledge of when the run stops
  - A variant uses $\log T$ instead, but then $T$ must be known in advance

// https://raw.githack.com/gpsaggese/gpsaggese.github.io/gp_scratch/msml610/tutorials/L09_multi_armed_bandits/L09_03_02_multi_armed_bandits.html#Cell-8:-UCB-Algorithm-Simulation
// https://raw.githack.com/gpsaggese/gpsaggese.github.io/gp_scratch/msml610/tutorials/L09_multi_armed_bandits/L09_03_02_multi_armed_bandits.html#Cell-9:-UCB-Exploration-Bonus-Decay

* UCB: Why It Works

- @Intuition@: the bonus $\sqrt{2 \log t / N_i(t)}$ is carefully calibrated
  - Derived from Hoeffding's concentration inequality
  - Ensures $\mu_i \leq U_i(t)$ with probability at least $1 - t^{-4}$
  - "Optimism": we act as if each arm is as good as its UCB

- @Properties@: how the two terms behave
  - Bonus $\propto 1/\sqrt{N_i}$: shrinks as an arm is pulled
  - Bonus $\propto \sqrt{\log t}$: grows slowly with time for _all_ arms
  - _Early_: all arms have few pulls $\to$ large bonuses $\to$ explores
  - _Middle_: focuses on promising arms, occasionally revisits uncertain ones
  - _Late_: mostly pulls optimal arm, bonus becomes negligible

- @Self-Correcting@
  - Pull a suboptimal arm too much: its $N_i$ grows, its UCB decreases
  - Neglect the optimal arm: $\log t$ grows while its $N_i$ stays fixed, so its
    UCB increases until it is pulled again
  - No arm can be abandoned forever

- @Remark@: UCB1 needs no tuning to be _order-optimal_
  - Unlike $\epsilon$-greedy, no schedule and no knowledge of $\Delta_i$
  - In practice the constant 2 in the bonus is often lowered (e.g., to 0.5),
    which reduces over-exploration at the cost of the theoretical guarantee

// https://raw.githack.com/gpsaggese/gpsaggese.github.io/gp_scratch/msml610/tutorials/L09_multi_armed_bandits/L09_03_02_multi_armed_bandits.html#Cell-8:-UCB-Algorithm-Simulation

* UCB: Worked Example

- @Example@: $K = 3$ Bernoulli arms with (unknown) $\mu = (0.3, 0.5, 0.7)$
  - Rounds $t = 1, 2, 3$ pull each arm once, giving rewards $1$, $1$, $0$
  - So after initialization $\hat{\mu} = (1.00, 1.00, 0.00)$

\begingroup \footnotesize
```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: (
    [$t$], [$U_1(t)$], [$U_2(t)$], [$U_3(t)$], [Pull], [$R_t$],
    [$hat(mu)$ after the pull]
  ),
  rows: (
    ([4], [2.67], [2.67], [1.67], [arm 1], [0], [(0.50, 1.00, 0.00)]),
    ([5], [1.77], [2.79], [1.79], [arm 2], [0], [(0.50, 0.50, 0.00)]),
    ([6], [1.84], [1.84], [1.89], [arm 3], [1], [(0.50, 0.50, 0.50)]),
    ([7], [1.89], [1.89], [1.89], [arm 1], [0], [(0.33, 0.50, 0.50)]),
    ([8], [1.51], [1.94], [1.94], [arm 2], [1], [(0.33, 0.67, 0.50)]),
    ([9], [1.54], [1.88], [1.98], [arm 3], [1], [(0.33, 0.67, 0.67)]),
  ),
  caption: "UCB1 trace, ties broken by lowest index",
  col-widths: (0.4fr, 0.8fr, 0.8fr, 0.8fr, 0.8fr, 0.5fr, 2.2fr),
  width: 100%,
  bold-first-col: true
)
```
\endgroup

- @Remark@: look at round $t = 6$
  - Arm 3 has the _worst_ empirical mean ($0.00$ vs $0.50$)
  - It is pulled anyway, because it has the largest bonus ($1.89$ vs $1.34$)
  - This single pull rescues the truly best arm from an unlucky start
  - By $t = 9$ arm 3 leads on the UCB index, which is the correct answer

* Regret Analysis of UCB

- @Theorem@ (Auer et al., 2002):
  - For UCB1 with rewards in $[0, 1]$, the expected cumulative regret satisfies
    $$\EE[L_T] \leq
    8 \sum_{i: \Delta_i > 0} \frac{\log T}{\Delta_i} +
    \left( 1 + \frac{\pi^2}{3} \right) \sum_{i=1}^{K} \Delta_i$$
  - Simplified expression:
    $$
    \EE[L_T] = O\left( \sum_{i: \Delta_i > 0} \frac{\log T}{\Delta_i} \right)
    $$

- @Interpretation@
  - Logarithmic regret: $\EE[L_T] = O(\log T)$
  - Matches the Lai-Robbins lower bound up to constants, so UCB1 is
    order-optimal
  - Cannot do better asymptotically in the gap-dependent sense

- @Remark@: the bound _looks_ unbounded as $\Delta_i \to 0$, but the true regret
  is not
  - Arm $i$ can never contribute more than $\Delta_i T$
  - Combining the two, UCB1 also satisfies the gap-free bound
    $\EE[L_T] = O(\sqrt{K T \log T})$
  - This is within $\sqrt{\log T}$ of the $\Omega(\sqrt{KT})$ minimax lower
    bound

* Variants of UCB

- @UCB1@: Original algorithm
  - Assumes bounded rewards in $[0, 1]$ and uses the same bonus for all arms

- @UCB-V@: Variance-Aware UCB (Audibert, Munos, Szepesvari, 2009)
  - Incorporates empirical variance of rewards
    $$
    U^{V}_i(t)
    = \hat{\mu}_i(t) + \sqrt{\frac{2 \hat{\sigma}_i^2(t) \log t}{N_i(t)}}
      + \frac{3 \log t}{N_i(t)}
    $$
    where $\hat{\sigma}_i^2(t)$ is sample variance of arm $i$
  - E.g., some ads have consistent CTR, others highly variable
  - **Benefit**: Better for arms with low variance (tighter bounds)

- @KL-UCB@ (Garivier and Cappe, 2011)
  - Uses Kullback-Leibler divergence for tighter confidence bounds
    $$
    U^{KL}_i(t)
    = \max \left\{ q : N_i(t) \, D(\hat{\mu}_i(t) \| q)
      \leq \log t + c \log \log t \right\}
    $$
    where $D(p \| q)$ is KL divergence
  - **Benefit**: matches the Lai-Robbins constant exactly, unlike UCB1

- @MOSS@ (Audibert and Bubeck, 2009)
  - Replaces $\log t$ with $\log(T / (K N_i(t)))$
  - **Benefit**: minimax-optimal $O(\sqrt{KT})$ gap-free regret

# Bayesian Approaches

// https://raw.githack.com/gpsaggese/gpsaggese.github.io/gp_scratch/msml610/tutorials/L09_multi_armed_bandits/L09_03_02_multi_armed_bandits.html#Cell-12:-Bayesian-Bandits:-Prior-and-Posterior

* Bayesian Bandits

- @Recap@: so far **frequentist** approach (e.g., UCB, $\epsilon$-greedy)
  - Treat $\mu_i$ as fixed unknown constants
  - Use confidence bounds and empirical means

- @Definition@: **Bayesian Approach** for multi-armed bandits:
  - Treat $\mu_i$ as random variables
  - Start with prior distribution $p(\mu_i)$ for each arm
  - Update beliefs using Bayes' rule after each observation
  - Make decisions based on posterior distributions

- @Components@: the Bayesian framework
  - Prior: $p(\mu_i)$
    - E.g., $\mu_i \sim Beta(1, 1)$ for Bernoulli arm
  - Likelihood: $p(R | \mu_i)$
    - E.g., $R \sim Bernoulli(\mu_i)$
  - Posterior (by Bayes' rule):
    $p(\mu_i | R_{1:t}) \propto p(R_{1:t} | \mu_i) \, p(\mu_i)$

// https://raw.githack.com/gpsaggese/gpsaggese.github.io/gp_scratch/msml610/tutorials/L09_multi_armed_bandits/L09_03_02_multi_armed_bandits.html#Cell-12:-Bayesian-Bandits:-Prior-and-Posterior

* Bayesian Bandits: Priors and Posteriors

- We can use the usual framework for Bayesian inference and decision making

- @Prior Distribution@: Encodes initial beliefs about $\mu_i$
  - Uniform prior: e.g., $\mu_i \sim Beta(1, 1)$ (no prior knowledge)
  - Informative prior: e.g., $\mu_i \sim Beta(\alpha, \beta)$ (domain knowledge)

- @Posterior Update@: After observing rewards from arm $i$
  - Suppose arm $i$ has been pulled $n$ times, with $s$ successes, $f$ failures
  - Posterior (for Bernoulli arm with Beta prior):
    $$p(\mu_i | \mathcal{D}) = Beta(\alpha + s, \beta + f)$$
  - Conjugate prior: Posterior has same form as prior (easy to update)

- @Bayesian Inference@: Posterior encodes all information about $\mu_i$
  - Mean:
    $\EE[\mu_i | \mathcal{D}] = \frac{\alpha + s}{\alpha + \beta + n}$
  - Uncertainty:
    $$
    \VV[\mu_i | \mathcal{D}]
      = \frac{(\alpha + s)(\beta + f)}
             {(\alpha + \beta + n)^2 (\alpha + \beta + n + 1)}
    $$
  - More data $\to$ sharper posterior (less uncertainty)

* Bayesian Decision-Making

- Several strategies on how to choose arm from the posteriors

- @Greedy Bayesian@: Choose arm with highest posterior mean
  $$A_t = \argmax_i \EE[\mu_i | \mathcal{D}_t]$$
  - _Problem_: No exploration $\to$ same issue as frequentist greedy

- @Bayes-UCB@ (Kaufmann, Cappe, Garivier, 2012): Use posterior quantile as UCB
  $$A_t = \argmax_i Q_i\left(1 - \frac{1}{t (\log T)^c}\right)$$
  where $Q_i(p)$ is the $p$-th quantile of posterior $p(\mu_i | \mathcal{D}_t)$
  - Similar to UCB, but uses Bayesian uncertainty

- @Probability of Best Arm@: Choose the arm most likely to be optimal
  $$
  A_t
    = \argmax_i \Pr\left(\mu_i = \max_j \mu_j \,\middle|\, \mathcal{D}_t\right)
  $$
  - _Problem_: this is a _deterministic_ rule, so it can lock in like greedy
  - Contrast with Thompson Sampling, which _samples_ arm $i$ with that same
    probability instead of always taking the $\argmax$

- @Thompson Sampling@: Sample from posteriors and play best sample (next slide)

// https://raw.githack.com/gpsaggese/gpsaggese.github.io/gp_scratch/msml610/tutorials/L09_multi_armed_bandits/L09_03_02_multi_armed_bandits.html#Cell-13:-Thompson-Sampling-Algorithm
// https://raw.githack.com/gpsaggese/gpsaggese.github.io/gp_scratch/msml610/tutorials/L09_multi_armed_bandits/L09_03_02_multi_armed_bandits.html#Cell-14:-Thompson-Sampling:-Probability-Matching

* Thompson Sampling

- @Definition@: **Thompson Sampling** (Thompson, 1933)
  - At each time $t$:
    1. Sample $\tilde{\mu}_i \sim p(\mu_i | \mathcal{D}_t)$ for each arm $i$
    2. Pull arm $A_t = \argmax_i \tilde{\mu}_i$
    3. Observe reward $R_t$
    4. Update posterior for arm $A_t$

- @Intuition@: "Probability matching"
  - Pull arm $i$ with probability exactly $\Pr(i = i^* | \mathcal{D}_t)$
  - If arm likely to be best, sample will often be highest
  - If arm uncertain, its posterior is wide, so it might still win a draw
    (exploration)

- @Example@: Bernoulli Bandits with Beta Priors
  - Posterior: $p(\mu_i | \mathcal{D}) = Beta(\alpha_i, \beta_i)$
  - Algorithm:
    1. Sample $\tilde{\mu}_i \sim Beta(\alpha_i, \beta_i)$ for each $i$
    2. Pull $A_t = \argmax_i \tilde{\mu}_i$
    3. If reward $R_t = 1$: $\alpha_{A_t} \gets \alpha_{A_t} + 1$;
       else $\beta_{A_t} \gets \beta_{A_t} + 1$

* Thompson Sampling: Posterior Evolution

- @Example@: 3 Bernoulli arms with $\mu = (0.30, 0.50, 0.55)$, Beta$(1,1)$
  priors

![](msml610/lectures_source/figures/L09.3.beta_posteriors.png){width=100%}

- @Interpretation@
  - _0 pulls_: flat priors, so every arm wins a draw equally often
  - _10 pulls_: the empirically best arm (arm 2) is _not_ the truly best one
    (arm 3), but sampling still picks arm 3 often, so the mistake is recoverable
  - _100 pulls_: arm 1 separates cleanly and stops being sampled
  - _1000 pulls_: almost all draws go to arm 3

- @Key idea@: the width of the posterior _is_ the exploration bonus
  - UCB adds it explicitly, Thompson Sampling gets it from the posterior draw

// https://raw.githack.com/gpsaggese/gpsaggese.github.io/gp_scratch/msml610/tutorials/L09_multi_armed_bandits/L09_03_02_multi_armed_bandits.html#Cell-15:-UCB-vs-Thompson-Sampling-Comparison

* Thompson Sampling: Practical Performance

- @Theoretical Guarantees@
  - Achieves $\EE[L_T] = O(\log T)$ gap-dependent regret (optimal)
  - Achieves $O(\sqrt{KT \log T})$ gap-free regret, matching UCB1
  - Proofs more involved than UCB (Agrawal and Goyal, 2012)

- @Empirical Performance@
  - Often _outperforms_ UCB in practice (better constants)
  - Faster convergence to optimal arm in many problems
  - More robust to model misspecification

- @Pros@
  - Naturally incorporates prior knowledge via priors
  - Flexible: Works with any reward distribution (if conjugate prior available)
  - Simple randomized algorithm (no complex optimization)
  - Randomization also makes it natural for _batched_ or _delayed_ feedback,
    where UCB would pull the same arm for the whole batch
  - _De facto_ standard in practice, especially industry

- @Cons@
  - Requires choosing prior (though often uniform prior works well)
  - Posterior updates may be non-trivial for complex distributions
  - Randomized, so runs are not reproducible without fixing a seed

# Putting It Together

// https://raw.githack.com/gpsaggese/gpsaggese.github.io/gp_scratch/msml610/tutorials/L09_multi_armed_bandits/L09_03_02_multi_armed_bandits.html#Cell-11:-Comparing-Algorithms:-Regret-Curves

* Regret Comparison: Empirical

- @Example@: 5 Bernoulli arms with $\mu = (0.9, 0.7, 0.5, 0.3, 0.1)$, regret
  averaged over 150 runs

![](msml610/lectures_source/figures/L09.3.regret_curves.png){width=100%}

- @Interpretation@
  - _Left_: greedy and fixed-$\epsilon$ are straight lines, i.e., $\Theta(T)$
  - _Right_: on a log-$t$ axis, UCB1, decaying $\epsilon_t$-greedy and Thompson
    Sampling are close to straight lines, i.e., $O(\log t)$

- @Remark@: greedy is _worse_ than fixed-$\epsilon$, even though it "wastes"
  nothing on exploration
  - A permanent commitment to a wrong arm costs more than 10% random pulls

// https://raw.githack.com/gpsaggese/gpsaggese.github.io/gp_scratch/msml610/tutorials/L09_multi_armed_bandits/L09_03_02_multi_armed_bandits.html#Cell-11:-Comparing-Algorithms:-Regret-Curves
// https://raw.githack.com/gpsaggese/gpsaggese.github.io/gp_scratch/msml610/tutorials/L09_multi_armed_bandits/L09_03_02_multi_armed_bandits.html#Cell-15:-UCB-vs-Thompson-Sampling-Comparison

* Regret Comparison: Summary Table

\begingroup \footnotesize
```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ([Algorithm], [Gap-dependent regret], [Exploration], [Tuning]),
  rows: (
    ([Greedy], [$Theta(T)$], [None], [None]),
    ([Uniform exploration], [$Theta(T)$], [Uniform, forever], [None]),
    ([Explore-then-commit], [$Theta(T^(2\/3))$, tuned],
     [Fixed budget, up front], [Budget $n$]),
    ([$epsilon$-greedy, fixed], [$Theta(T)$], [Uniform, forever],
     [$epsilon$]),
    ([$epsilon$-greedy, decaying], [$O(log T)$ if gap known],
     [Uniform, shrinking], [Schedule]),
    ([UCB1], [$O(log T)$], [Directed by uncertainty], [None]),
    ([KL-UCB], [$O(log T)$, optimal constant], [Directed by uncertainty],
     [None]),
    ([Thompson sampling], [$O(log T)$], [Posterior sampling], [Prior]),
    ([Lower bound], [$Omega(log T)$], [-], [-]),
  ),
  caption: [Minimax lower bound is $Omega(sqrt(K T))$; UCB1 and Thompson
    achieve $O(sqrt(K T log T))$],
  col-widths: (1.3fr, 1.4fr, 1.3fr, 0.8fr),
  width: 100%,
  bold-first-col: true
)
```
\endgroup

- @Practical Takeaway@: UCB and Thompson Sampling dominate the simpler
  heuristics for stationary problems
  - Between the two, Thompson Sampling usually wins empirically, UCB is easier
    to analyze and is deterministic

// https://raw.githack.com/gpsaggese/gpsaggese.github.io/gp_scratch/msml610/tutorials/L09_multi_armed_bandits/L09_03_02_multi_armed_bandits.html#Cell-15:-UCB-vs-Thompson-Sampling-Comparison

* Which Algorithm Should I Use?

- @Stationary, few arms, Bernoulli or bounded rewards@
  - Start with **Thompson Sampling** with $Beta(1,1)$ priors
  - Best empirical performance, trivial to implement, handles batching

- @You need determinism or auditability@
  - Use **UCB1** or **KL-UCB**
  - No randomness, so decisions are reproducible and explainable
  - Required in some regulated settings (e.g., clinical trials)

- @Rewards depend on user or item features@
  - Use a **contextual bandit** (LinUCB, contextual Thompson Sampling), L09.7

- @Reward distributions drift over time@
  - Use a **non-stationary** method (sliding-window UCB, discounted UCB),
    L09.7

- @No stochastic model is credible (prices, adversaries, spam)@
  - Use an **adversarial** algorithm (EXP3), L09.7

- @You only care about finding the best arm, not about reward during the test@
  - This is **best-arm identification**, not regret minimization, L09.7

- @Remark@: if you cannot adapt traffic at all, a classic A/B test (ETC) is
  still a valid, if suboptimal, choice
